  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Fog likely tonight, especially Downeast. Fog \ncould be dense enough to impact travel especially near the \ncoast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nFog has mostly lifted this afternoon, with the notable exception\nof coastal Hancock County. The fog initially lifted this \nmorning for coastal Hancock, but low clouds and fog have moved \nback in early this afternoon as onshore flow increases. Less fog\nright now for Washington County coast, but that should change\nthis evening. Think that the Downeast coast could be in store \nfor dense fog again tonight, with very moist low-level onshore \nflow persisting. Not quite confident enough yet to issue dense \nfog advisory for the coast, but it's definitely something to \nwatch. Further inland tonight, while there will be some fog, \nthink that the tendency will be for it to be mainly low clouds a\ncouple hundred feet off the ground. Do have patchy fog in the \nforecast for all areas though.\n\nFog risk lessens some during the day Thursday and into Thursday\nnight despite some light rain. This is because of the low-level\nflow becoming more easterly instead of southerly, which means a\nbit less low-level moisture coming off the Gulf of Maine. Still\ncould be some coastal fog during the day Thursday and possibly\nanywhere Thursday night, but not anticipating anything too bad.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Periods of light rain late tonight, Thursday, \nand Thursday night, with higher totals Downeast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure passing south of the area will graze us with some\nlight rain. This is not looking impactful in terms of flooding,\nand will be beneficial for many areas as it is getting fairly\ndry. In fact, there are some river and streams that are running\nclose to record low levels for the date. The greatest rain \ntotals from this system will very likely be Downeast. There is \nstill a lot of uncertainty as to just how much rain makes it \nnorth of Bangor. Generally, models are trending a little wetter \nfurther north, and we are trending the forecast this direction.\nIf rain does make it to far Northern Maine, most likely it \nwould be during the day Thursday, ending Thursday evening. \nDowneast has a larger window of possible light rain from this \nevening through Thursday night, but it will be intermittent and \nnot raining even close to the whole time. All together, we are \nlooking at anywhere from a trace to quarter inch of rain for \nNorthern Maine, a tenth to third of an inch central areas, and a\nquarter to three quarters of an inch Downeast. There could a \ncouple embedded storms during the day Thursday Downeast and over\nthe waters, but these will not be surface-based and not strong.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Chance of rain and thunderstorms this weekend\ninto early next week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\nSome isolated showers with general thunder possible in the North\nWoods on Saturday. Low pressure system approaches from the west\nSunday into Monday, bringing the next chance at steady rainfall.\nLow pressure swings into Maine as a high pressure system to the\nsouthwest pushes northeast into the Great Lakes region. Not\nanticipating heavy rain totals right now  could see up to 12 an\ninch in north, and up to 14 of an inch Downeast. There could be\nsome thunder moving along frontal boundary on Monday, with a few\nhundred J/kg of CAPE and 7-8C/km lapse rates. Too early to have\nconfidence in any storm severity, however.",
